{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nTeams running several tracker projects against one codebase, or several repos against one shared ticket store, could not use the bridge safely because the Jira bridge was historically single-project: `JiraConfig.project` was scalar, inbound fetch was scoped to one key, and outbound apply rejected targets outside that one project. The epic's original scope was to support many-to-many tracker projects over one shared store while preserving legacy single-project behavior and preventing cross-project contamination.\n\n## Closeout summary\n\nThe implementation scope is complete on `main` through closed children:\n\n- `centaurial-jealous-bittern`: `bridge projects` mapping surface across CLI/library/MCP.\n- `magic-ice-mouse`: inbound per-project JQL fan-out and source-project recording.\n- `reptilian-sheepish-dunlin`: tri-state `bridge_project`/`repos` ticket fields and empty-project semantics.\n- `obscene-hygienic-copperhead`: outbound per-ticket project dispatch and set-membership guard.\n- `senile-larval-hoverfly`: legacy-store/event-free ensure migration.\n- `dowdy-fretful-manta`: conditional `multi-project-bridge` store-compat capability.\n- `unpleasant-guardable-quelea`: docs and ADR 0062.\n- Size-relief prerequisite children are closed; duplicate residual `missing-creeded-wolfspider` was closed obsolete because the same extraction landed via `bloated-shadeful-lamprey`.\n- Live rehearsal child `initial-slothful-tick` was remediated and closed obsolete under the operator's closeout authorization: the original production behavior is implemented and tested by the closed children, while a one-off live Jira/S3 rehearsal is disproportionate operational work for epic closeout.\n\n## Verification\n\nCloseout verification is tracker and code-history based: all twelve descendants are closed, the production behavior maps to the closed implementation children listed above, and the two residual closeout children have recorded obsolete/no-code dispositions. No new code change is required for this epic closeout.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] `bridge projects` lists, sets and removes project→repos entries on the store mapping, and the same operations exist on the library and MCP surfaces.\n  provenance: closed child `centaurial-jealous-bittern`.\n- [x] A store whose mapping holds two projects fetches both in a single pass and each ingested ticket records the project it came from.\n  provenance: closed child `magic-ice-mouse`.\n- [x] A ticket whose project is explicitly empty produces no outbound mutation.\n  provenance: closed child `reptilian-sheepish-dunlin`.\n- [x] A ticket created with an explicit project targets that project rather than the repo default.\n  provenance: closed child `obscene-hygienic-copperhead`.\n- [x] An outbound update targeting a project inside the configured set proceeds while one outside it still raises `CrossProjectTargetError`.\n  provenance: closed child `obscene-hygienic-copperhead`.\n- [x] Every ticket in a pre-feature store resolves to the recorded legacy default with zero events written.\n  provenance: closed child `senile-larval-hoverfly`.\n- [x] A store whose mapping holds more than one project carries `multi-project-bridge` in `.store-compat.json` `required_capabilities` and a binary lacking that capability refuses writes, while a single-project store remains writable.\n  provenance: closed child `dowdy-fretful-manta`.\n"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788892531905113001,"uuid":"f978f171-ddfc-4281-ab29-fc542e9d16a8"}